Without rules, anyone with the app's config can read or write your database. Start from deny-all and add narrow allowances:
match /users/{uid}/notes/{id} {
 allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;
}
Validate data shape in rules (request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly([...])), never trust fields like role that the client sets, and test with the Firebase emulator before deploying. The Firebase config values in the app are identifiers, not secrets; rules and server-side checks are the security.

A component is a function that returns what a piece of the screen looks like. State (useState) is data the component remembers. When state changes, React re-runs the function and updates the screen.
const [likes, setLikes] = useState(0);
<Pressable onPress={() => setLikes(likes + 1)}><Text>{likes}</Text></Pressable>

useEffect(() => { ... }, [dependencies]) runs after render. Typical use: fetch from your API when a screen opens.
useEffect(() => {
 fetch(`${API}/api/posts`).then(r => r.json()).then(setPosts);
}, []);
The [] means "only once". Leave it off and the effect runs after every render; if it sets state, that triggers another render, and you get an infinite loop.

A relational database (MySQL, SQLite) stores rows in tables with a fixed set of columns. MongoDB stores documents: JSON-like objects that can nest and differ from each other. Documents live in collections, collections live in a database.
{ "_id": 1, "name": "Ava", "roles": ["admin", "dev"], "address": { "city": "Baton Rouge" } }
Rough translation: table = collection, row = document, column = field, JOIN = $lookup (or just embed the data).

Stages run in order, each feeding the next:
db.orders.aggregate([
 { $match: { status: "paid" } },
 { $group: { _id: "$customer", total: { $sum: "$amount" } } },
 { $sort: { total: -1 } }, { $limit: 5 }
])

No SQL strings, but you can still inject. If the server passes the request body straight into a query, an attacker sends operators instead of values:
{ "user": "admin", "pass": { "$ne": "" } }
becomes find({ user: "admin", pass: { $ne: "" } }): "password is not empty", so any password matches. $gt, $regex and $where do similar damage. Defenses: validate that fields are plain strings, strip keys starting with $ (or use express-mongo-sanitize), set the schema type (String), never use $where with user input, and never store plain passwords (hash with bcrypt/argon2).

public class Account {
 private final String id;
 private long cents;
 public Account(String id, long cents) { this.id = id; this.cents = cents; }
 public void withdraw(long amount) {
 if (amount <= 0 || amount > cents) throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad amount");
 cents -= amount;
 }
 public long balance() { return cents; }
}
There is no setter for cents, so every change goes through a method that enforces the rules. That one design choice prevents a large class of bugs and security flaws.

